  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nSurface high pressure is centered overhead across the western \nGreat Lakes early this morning. Light winds under this high may \nsupport patchy fog development before dawn, particularly west- \nsouthwest of the Chicago metro area. However, the main threat \nfor dense fog will remain to the southwest across portions of \nnorthern Missouri into west-central Illinois. Any fog that \ndevelops will quickly burn off after sunrise.\n\nThe western Great Lakes surface high will slide eastward into \nNew England through tonight. As it does, the surface synoptic \nflow will favor a south-southeasterly direction. However, wind \ndirections are likely to turn more easterly across northeastern\nIllinois this afternoon as the flow becomes augmented by a lake\nbreeze. Overall, today will be a pleasant early fall day for \nthe area. Mostly sunny skies should warm inland locations to \naround (or just above) 80\u00b0F this afternoon, with slightly cooler\nconditions expected lakeside. Humidity levels are expected to \nremain comfortable.\n\nWarm air advection is forecast to ramp up across the area \nFriday night into Saturday as the low-level synoptic flow shifts\nsouth-southwesterly ahead of an approaching cold frontal \ntrough. This will support a temporary bump in temperatures (into\nthe mid-80s) and humidity on Saturday. The front should slide \nacross the area late Saturday afternoon and evening. Currently, \nrain chances with this front look low (<20%) due to weak frontal\nand larger-scale forcing.\n\nNortherly low-level flow behind Saturday evening's cold front \nwill push a cooler air mass across the region for Sunday. \nIncreased cloud cover and a chance of afternoon showers tied to \nan approaching mid-level impulse should keep high temperatures \nin the 70s across most locations. Rain chances then look to \nshift south and southwest of the area Sunday night as surface \nhigh pressure builds over the Great Lakes. While the current \ngridded forecast maintains 20-40% shower chances Sunday night, \nthese may need to be lowered or removed if guidance trends hold.\n \nThe first half of next week continues to look active across the\nregion. Upper-level ridging will expand eastward over the \nsoutheastern CONUS while a shortwave trough axis migrates east- \nnortheastward across the northern tier. As this occurs, a \ntrailing surface cold front is expected to become quasi- \nstationary somewhere over the lower Great Lakes region through \nmidweek, favoring multiple rounds of heavy rain-producing \nshowers and thunderstorms. This pattern could result in a Maddox\nfrontal heavy rain setup Tuesday into Wednesday, so this period\nwill need to be monitored closely over the coming days.\n\nKJB",
